A||N||7||atarian||2.5 TBI races everytime I start it||||1||
Z||000000||atarian||08-10-2001||04:48 AM||||Hi, hope someone can help. I have a 87' 2.5L(without cruise control) that races at 3k rpm everytime i start it. if i drive it, the engine will race but if i let it sit like at a stop sign it will go down to a normal operating idle within one minute. Also I have replaced the oil pan gasket twice. it is very difficult to get to. i can't stop it from leaking any suggestions?||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||08-10-2001||04:27 PM||||idle problem that will come down on its own, sounds like it could be the coolant temp sensor for ecm.||||reg||1
Z||000002||x-thumpr-x||08-10-2001||04:33 PM||||Have you replaced you TPS sensor lately? I had the same probelm on my 84. I replaced the TPS, IAC & MAP sensor. Not all at once, but after, my idle went down to normal. The car will run around 2000rpm for a short bit before it drops down to a low idle. (have you been getting any codes?)<BR>As for your oil pan, have you cleaned the old oil pan gasket off completely before puttin on the new one?||||reg||1
Z||000003||EdsB52||08-10-2001||05:07 PM||||Sounds to me like the IAC (Idle Air Control) valve. This is an electric valve located on the left side of the throttle body with a square 4 pin plug. This valve costs about $40 at autozone. The function of the IAC is to establish idle speed and possibly mixture (?). It has a plunger in it that extends or retracts based on input from the computer. When the car is operating with this IAC that has a plunger with a conical valve end, a small clearance exists between the valve and the seat inside the throttle body. As the computer sends input to the IAC it varies that clearance, allowing more or less outside air into the throttle body. If the plunger moves too far to the retracted position, it acts as if a major vacuum line fell off, hence revving the engine.<P>If/when you replace this valve, be careful that the new plunger isn't extended more than the old one that you removed. If it is and you force it into position, you may damage the new plunger mechanism. Also, if you replace the IAC, it may run the same way until you drive over 40mph and it sets itself. I've replaced these and actually had to drive around for a while before it set itself as opposed to the one time to 40mph. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000004||My7Fieros||08-10-2001||05:40 PM||||More than likely a simple vacuum leak...check for a broken line...no biggie.||||reg||1
Z||000005||EdsB52||08-10-2001||06:38 PM||||The only vacuum line that would cause the engine to rev that much would be the one going to the brake booster.||||reg||1
Z||000006||dman||08-10-2001||06:38 PM||||Hello everyone. Actually thats normal! Yup, thats right. A lot of 2.5 motors do that. Search the archives for a discussion about this. The ECM apparently tells the IAC to do exactly that, the idle should come down on its own within a minute or two. Its only if you drive immediately after starting does it continue to race.<P>Where is it leaking? Since you've changed the gasket I'm surprised it still leaks. But usually it will be the areas by the rear main bearing cap or where the pan meets the front timing cover. You need to put lots of RTV where those different gasket pieces meet.<BR>If your bearing cap and timing cover didn't have the grooves than you probably had to throw out the rubber gasket pieces in the kit and just use plain RTV, in which case you need to make sure you use enough to completely form a leak proof barrier.<P>Of course its always possible that your rear main seal is whats leaking, and not the pan. I'm pretty sure you'd know that by cleaning it up and then checking for the first sign of a leak and examining where it came from.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-10-2001||06:50 PM||||EGR problem or Vac leak is a good posibility. electrical problem, maybe. I doubt the IAC, but it's posible. <P>87-88 TPS is NON adjustable.<P>ECT, TPS, and MAT can all be checked in about 5 minutes with an ECM scanner and a thermometer. MAP is scanable but you'd need a conversion table for the info. <P>Or you can blow about $100 and just replace them all. Maybe you fix it that way, maybe you don't....||||reg||1
